Great catch — the key is right! The problem says **compounded annually**, so we must use the compound‑interest formula, not I = Prt.

\[
A = P\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)^{nt}
      = 500\left(1+\frac{0.04}{1}\right)^{1\cdot 3}
      = 500(1.04)^{3}
\]

\[
(1.04)^{3}=1.124864\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;A = 500 \times 1.124864 = \$562.432 \approx \$562.43
\]

The extra \$2.43 comes from earning interest on the interest each year. Remember to check whether the problem says “simple” or “compound” interest before picking a formula. Good luck tomorrow!

